      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Primary ciliary dyskinesia (PCD) is an inherited disease characterized by specific ultrastructural defects of cilia and sperms. The impairment of mucociliary clearance (MCC) results in chronic respiratory infections and subsequently in bronchiectasis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "MAIN PURPOSE",
          "content": "The evaluate rational decisions in early diagnosis of PCD."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Samples of nasal mucosa or tissue of tonsilla pharyngea after adenotomy were studied by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) in 47 patients aged 1-15 years, suffering from recurrent or chronic respiratory infections."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Congenital ultrastructural ciliary defects specific for PCD--the lack of dynein arms, radial spokes defects and microtubular transposition--were observed in 13 patients. TEM investigation is an expansive, time consuming method not available in routine practice. Therefore we have evaluated a diagnostic procedure which uses available examination methods focused on the diagnoses of PCD. TEM of respiratory cilia is indicated in patients with situs viscerum solitus if chronic respiratory disease develops and after more frequent causes--asthma, cystic fibrosis, congenital anomalies of respiratory system and immunodeficiency had been excluded."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The correct and early diagnosis is important for effective therapy in order to improve MCC. This approach can prevent the development of bronchiectasis during childhood."
        }
      ]},
